{
  "schema": "zte_tpd_async_lifecycle_hardening_v1",
  "review_date": "2026-11-02",
  "baseline_commit": "49afe51ddcc0f4e11bd84ffc96987e36f71d5cbb",
  "finding": {
    "risk": "queue_use_after_workqueue_destroy",
    "stock_sequence": [
      "syna_dev_remove cancels, flushes, and destroys the helper workqueue",
      "only afterward it removes panel/sysfs entry points and calls syna_cdev_remove",
      "syna_cdev_remove later calls syna_dev_disconnect, which releases the IRQ"
    ],
    "evidence": [
      "The Ghidra stock pseudocode for syna_dev_remove preserves this order.",
      "syna_dev_process_unexpected_reset queues work at tcm+1320 on the helper queue at tcm+1352.",
      "syna_dev_helper_work can requeue delayed reflash work on the separate queue at tcm+1072."
    ],
    "conclusion": "An IRQ callback could enqueue helper work after its queue was destroyed; helper work could also race the later destruction of its reflash queue."
  },
  "fix": {
    "changes": [
      "Quiesce cdev operations and remove panel/sysfs entry points before worker teardown.",
      "Use one IRQ-safe spinlock to serialize reset callback queueing, helper workqueue detachment, delayed reflash requeueing, and pending-flag completion.",
      "Detach the helper workqueue pointer under that lock, then cancel, flush, and destroy the saved queue. Call syna_cdev_remove afterward so disconnect stops the IRQ and destroys the separate reflash queue only after helper work has drained.",
      "Mark syna_dev_remove, syna_dev_helper_work, and syna_dev_process_unexpected_reset as HARDENED_STOCK_DEVIATION in reconstruction_map.json and MICROTASKS.md."
    ],
    "exact_parity_claimed": false
  },
